{align:justify|That day every newspaper carried the same headline: "Young doctor Truong Vi Luc takes his own life"... and readers could hardly believe it.}
{align:justify|Doctor Truong was 29 years old this year; after graduating last year, he had been serving at a government hospital.}
{align:justify|Doctor Truong came from a wealthy family, with kind and good-hearted parents. His three elder sisters were all married, and as the youngest child — and only son — he was doted on ten thousandfold. He was strikingly handsome, a quick-witted charmer who set a great many young hearts aflame.}
{align:justify|With a velvet-paved road stretching out before him, relatives and friends all envied his golden fortune — which is why no one could believe he had killed himself.}
{align:justify|The hospital therefore at once assembled a forensic team to investigate his suicide, starting from Truong Vi Luc's family background and his six years of medical school, right through to his graduation and service at the hospital. The documented file ran to more than 500 pages — proof of how deeply his superiors and colleagues cared about his death.}
{align:justify|Among the reports was one passage that read: "Only in the past year did people begin to notice that Truong Vi Luc's bold, virile, masculine bearing had suddenly changed, and that his gestures, actions, and voice... seemed in some ways feminine. Everyone assumed... it was probably because he had grown up in a house with three elder sisters and worked constantly alongside female nurses..."}
{align:justify|When his body was examined, doctors said the female hormones in him were excessively dominant, utterly overwhelming his manhood.}
{align:justify|In the file of Doctor Ngo Uy Xuong, who had treated him, it was further noted: "Patient Truong Vi Luc cannot absorb male hormones to maintain balance, and even suffers from 'castration-like impotence' (loss of manhood)." The file went on to record that he had suffered this "castrated" condition for a very long time.}
{align:justify|The investigation showed his family background was excellent, and that he himself was pure, proud, and conservative by nature. Though he left his parents young to work on his own, living alone amid the temptations of a glittering metropolis like Bangkok, and though his workplace was surrounded by beautiful women, he kept himself as spotless as jade — truly rare and admirable.}
{align:justify|Another valuable research report stated: "Truong Vi Luc was from southern Thailand. He passed the entrance examination to the Bangkok Medical University with honors, and during all six years of medical school he lived in a rented house near campus. Every day for breakfast he liked coffee and fruit juice."}
{align:justify|Most important of all: "At noon and in the evening he always ate chicken rice — the famous Hainan chicken rice that Thai people love so much. No green vegetables, just chicken with rice: a quick meal, convenient and tasty. Truong Vi Luc was especially fond of wings and heads, and he ate them steadily for six years"...}
{align:justify|An even more valuable finding: "In the past, farm-raised chickens took at least six or seven months before they were sold. But now, thanks to scientific farming, it takes only 40 days for a chicken to grow to full market weight. In olden days, a rooster raised to adolescence could not grow fat and plump, so to make it tastier people castrated it, making the flesh rich and fatty. With modern progress, science has invented drugs that rob chickens and other poultry of their reproductive capacity. Such drugs are made into pellets and implanted in the comb or the wing muscle; they are also mixed into the feed."}
{align:justify|So while we savor fattened capon meat, little do we know that we are swallowing those very "castrating" drugs into our own bellies — no wonder so many men today suffer "castration-like impotence"! It all comes from eating capons and gelded pigs.}
{align:justify|"Truong Vi Luc was especially fond of chicken wings and heads — yet those very parts are where the drugs concentrate. The first harm this medicine does is to suppress all male hormones; that 'castrating' drug seeped into Truong Vi Luc's body continuously for six straight years. These chemicals silently accumulated and took effect until they amounted to a horrifying, massive dose. In the end, even Doctor Truong Vi Luc could not cure his own 'castrated' condition."}
{align:justify|What joy is left in being alive, then? That was the preliminary conclusion about the cause of Doctor Truong's suicide. It happened, of course, without his knowing — but based on the Truong family background, the investigators obtained one more shocking report:}
{align:justify|It turned out that Truong Vi Luc's grandfather had spent his whole life castrating pigs and chickens back in their home village. His father had even followed the old man at the trade for two or three years before, unwilling to inherit his father's "castrating" profession, he abandoned it. Who could have known that the act of inflicting extinction on other creatures would bring calamity upon the third generation, driving the grandson to suicide and leaving the line without issue? Who would still dare disbelieve that this is karmic retribution?}
